# Slice 335 (Codex's answer to the 334 questions, item 3; the book :426): one result requirement is one value of the
# contract whether written by position, by head or by `:=` -- for a formal taken as written (name, return) the value
# of a part is its written field, not the block that holds it. p1, p2 and p3 all require `@: c.FILE`; a copy of each
# (merge with one more field) shows the same part return: a reference to that written type form. The executors run
# only int results, so none of the three is called here.
# Plan M11 5v: R1, R2, R3 are the places `@: R1 merge(...)` (they were `R1: merge(...)`); the root declares no ordinary
# Structure.
fn: p1 (int: a) @: c.FILE
    return: 0
fn: name := p2; args := (int: a); return := (@: c.FILE); code := (return: 0)
fn: name: p3; args: (int: a); return: (@: c.FILE); code: (return: 0)
@: R1 merge(p1; (int: zz 1))
@: R2 merge(p2; (int: zz 2))
@: R3 merge(p3; (int: zz 3))
